  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "When is the law of sines suitable to use?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "When two angles and one side are known, or when two sides and the angle opposite one of them are known.",
    "B": "Only to find the magnitude of a vector.",
    "C": "To replace every use of the law of cosines.",
    "D": "It holds only for equilateral triangles."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The law of sines relates a side to the sine of its opposite angle. For a nondegenerate triangle with sides \\(a,b\\) opposite angles \\(A,B\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{\\sin A}=\\frac{b}{\\sin B}.\n\\]\nWith two angles and one side, the \\(180^\\circ\\) angle sum determines the third angle, so the known side can be paired with its opposite angle. With two sides and an angle opposite one of them, such a pair is already known. These configurations allow us to find a missing side or another angle's sine. The answer is A.",
